# frozen_string_literal: true

=begin

  logs the game to <Lich log folder>\<game code>-<char name>\<year>\<month>\<date>_<time>.log
  (.xml in XML mode, see --xml below;
  the Lich log folder is LOG_DIR, set by Lich's --logs= option, or lich\logs;
  a file started by a new day or a full file whose name is already taken ends in _01, _02, ... before .log or .xml;
  a restarted logger appends to a file of the same name)
  starts a new file after 30,000 lines (somewhere around 1mb)

  SYNTAX:  ;log
           ;log --timestamp="%F %T %Z"          - optional timestamps
           ;log --rnum                          - optional show Lich ID on room titles
           ;log --exclude=u7199,288,2300        - optional rooms to exclude logging, supports UIDs via u#
                                                  (for a Lich room number, here and for --rnum, the logger
                                                  loads the map in the background when it is not loaded yet;
                                                  lines read before the load finishes are not excluded by it)
           ;log --lines=50000                   - optional change default(30000) lines to capture before new file started
           ;log --xml                           - XML mode: log the raw XML the game sends, to a .xml file, as ;logxml does
                                                  (typed commands as <!-- CLIENT -->command<!-- ENDCLIENT -->, the reget
                                                  as the server sent it, a 12-hour time in the first line); the options
                                                  above work in XML mode too; the word xml, without --, does the same
           ;log --xml streams                   - XML mode keeping the lines that start with pushStream or popStream,
                                                  which it drops otherwise (very noisy); --streams does the same
  TEXT AND XML TOGETHER:
           Lich runs one ;log at a time, so start ;log for the text and ;logxml for the XML: logxml.lic runs
           this script in XML mode under its own name, and takes the same options
           (logxml.lic reads the version line below, an indented "version:" and the version, before it loads
           this script, so keep that line's layout)
  EXAMPLE AUTOSTART:
           ;autostart add --global log --timestamp=\"%F %T %Z\" --rnum --exclude=288,2300,u7199 --lines=50000

           log format based on https://apidock.com/ruby/DateTime/strftime

       author: elanthia-online
 contributors: Tillmen, Tysong, Xanlin
         game: gs
      version: 1.3.1
     required: Lich >= 5.19.0

  changelog:
    1.3.1 (2026-11-10):
      * XML mode --rnum adds no " (u<uid>)" in GemStone when the room title line already shows the room's "(<uid>)", as text mode does (logxml.lic 0.6.6 wrote both); text and XML mode number room titles with one shared helper
      * XML mode --rnum numbers DragonRealms Saga room titles (<room><name>[...]</name>), and the other room title forms Lich numbers for its own room display (no picture resource, or popBold first); a familiar's view of its room and a title after a line break are left as sent
    1.3.0 (2026-11-10):
      * XML mode, with --xml or when started as ;logxml (logxml.lic now runs this script): the raw XML the game sends goes to a .xml file, as logxml.lic 0.6.6 wrote it (the reget as the server sent it, unstamped; typed commands as <!-- CLIENT -->command<!-- ENDCLIENT -->; a 12-hour time in the first line; lines starting with pushStream or popStream dropped unless streams or --streams is given; --rnum on the room name line)
      * --timestamp, --rnum, --exclude and --lines read and work the same in XML mode as in text mode
      * the log line saying typed commands are no longer logged, and the Lich log lines about a footer or a close that failed, start with the name the logger runs as (log or logxml)
    1.2.8 (2026-11-10):
      * needs Lich 5.19.0 or later: GameLogger::Mode is a Ruby 3.2 Data, and 5.19.0 is the first Lich that requires a Ruby that has it (Lich 5.14 to 5.18 accept Ruby 2.6)
      * each log run is now a GameLogger::Logger of its own, and what a log format decides (file extension, header, typed-command lines, which lines are dropped, reget lines, room title test, start message) is one GameLogger::Mode value; the log files and messages are unchanged
      * a log run started after log.lic was updated in the same session gets the new Logger and Mode, and a run already logging keeps the ones it started with, and with them the file, room and typed-command helpers it calls while it logs (now methods of GameLogger::Logger, such as GameLogger::Logger.with_log_file, instead of GameLogger); a run starting while another evaluates log.lic gets the old definitions or the new ones, never a missing one
    1.2.7 (2026-11-10):
      * remove GameLogger::Opts.as_list and the Opts.method_missing behind it, which nothing called; GameLogger::Opts.parse is unchanged
      * the reget lines at the top of the log are no longer checked for stream and dialog tags, which Lich's reget has already removed for this logger; a reget line of game text the server sent escaped that starts with "<" (such as "<pushStream") is now kept there, while the lines after the reget still drop it
      * drop the start-up checks for an old Lich and for script trust, which can no longer fail (this version required Lich 5.14.0 or later, which needs Ruby 2.6 or later)
    1.2.6 (2026-11-10):
      * when typed commands stop being logged after an error, the logger echoes the error, the file and line where it happened, and how to restart it with the same options, and the log file gets a line saying typed commands are not logged after it
      * a log file that cannot be closed goes to the Lich log with its path and the error, instead of a bare "Can't close file!"
    1.2.5 (2026-11-10):
      * a log run reads its options when it starts and keeps them to itself, so another log run starting at the same time (;force log) can no longer give it its --exclude, --rnum, --timestamp or --lines
    1.2.4 (2026-11-10):
      * the reget lines at the top of the log no longer end in a stray carriage return, and an empty reget writes nothing
      * logs go to Lich's log folder (LOG_DIR, set by Lich's --logs= option), or LICH_DIR/logs when it is not set
      * a log file now also gets its footer when the logger is stopped (;kill, logout, Lich exiting), not only when a new file starts; a footer that cannot be written then goes to the Lich log
      * a log file whose header cannot be written is closed instead of being left open
      * the new day's file starts with the first line read after midnight, which went into yesterday's file; a line in an --exclude room also starts it, and is still not logged
      * only lines written to the file count toward --lines, so excluded and filtered lines no longer make header-and-footer-only files
      * a file started by a new day or a full file whose name is taken (for example, a whole file within one second) gets an _01, _02, ... suffix (sorting after the first file) instead of being appended to it; the first file of a run still appends to a file of the same name
      * after a full file, the next file is opened when its first line comes, so no file is left without lines when that line comes after midnight or the logger stops first
    1.2.3 (2026-11-10):
      * --rnum and --exclude find the Lich room number in the map's UID table, never by matching the room from the logger (no LOCATION/PEER sent, no lost lines)
      * with --rnum or a Lich room number in --exclude, the logger loads the map once in the background when nothing has loaded it yet (in DragonRealms, Lich's own room display does not load it by default); until the map has the room's UID, each line asks again, so the room is numbered and excluded from then on
      * a room title with letters outside ASCII no longer stops the logger with an encoding error under --rnum
      * needs Lich 5.14.0 or later (Map.loaded?)
      * --rnum works in DragonRealms
      * --rnum writes no " - ]" when the room has no Lich number, and changes nothing before the first room title
      * --exclude ignores empty entries (--exclude=,288) and a bare --exclude
    1.2.2 (2026-11-10):
      * --timestamp keeps commas and "=" in the format; a --timestamp with no format echoes and is ignored instead of crashing
      * log_timestamp on with no usable log_timestamp_format echoes and uses "%F %T %Z" instead of crashing
      * --lines accepts 50,000 and 50_000; an invalid value echoes a message and uses 30000
      * an argument "--" with no option name echoes and is ignored instead of crashing
    1.2.1 (2026-09-25):
      * Expand regex match for various XML that may come across due to script output
    1.2.0 (2026-04-23):
      * Use --timestamp format for log file header/footer lines when enabled
    1.1.0 (2026-04-23):
      * Timestamp reget lines with $login_time instead of dumping them raw
      * Filter pushStream/popStream tags from reget output
    1.0.0 (2025-04-08):
      * Fix --rnum to better match only room titles
      * Add --exclude=288,u7199 support to exclude logging in rooms designated.
      * Add --lines=50000 to change default 30k lines captured before starting new log file
      * Convert to module GameLogger
=end
=begin
    0.0.8 (2024-09-10):
      * Add --rnum support
    0.0.7 (2023-03-31):
      * Rubocop code cleanup
      * Adjust filename to include hours, minutes, seconds
      * Save into subdirectories by year/month
      * Starts a new file when the day changes
      * Add log timestamp CLI option for non-DragonRealms
    0.0.6 (2023-01-23):
      * Update for Ruby v3 compatibility
    0.0.5 (2022-11-24):
      * Remove reference to $SAFE
    0.0.4 (2022-01-10):
      * Making datetime stamps customizeable, and tuneable in a character's yaml.
    0.0.3 (2022-01-04):
      * prepend datetime stamps to logged lines
    0.0.2 (2015-01-13):
      * create log directory if needed
=end

hide_me

module GameLogger
  # Lines written to one log file before a new file is started, when
  # --lines is not given or is not a valid count.
  DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE ||= 30_000

  # Timestamp format used when the settings turn timestamps on but give no
  # usable log_timestamp_format (the DR base.yaml default).
  DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT ||= '%F %T %Z'

  # A --lines value: plain digits, or groups of three digits after the first
  # group, split by "," or "_" (50000, 50,000, 50_000, 1,000,000).
  LINE_COUNT_PATTERN ||= /\A(?:[0-9]+|[0-9]{1,3}(?:[,_][0-9]{3})+)\z/

  module Opts
    FLAG_PREFIX ||= "--"
    # Flags whose value is a comma-separated list. Every other flag keeps its
    # value whole, so a --timestamp format may contain commas.
    LIST_FLAGS ||= %i[exclude].freeze

    # Records a positional word as a +true+ option. A word never replaces a
    # value already given for the same name (the word +timestamp+ after
    # --timestamp=FMT keeps FMT).
    #
    # @param h [Hash{Symbol => Object}] options parsed so far; updated in place
    # @param c [String] the positional word, e.g. "streams"
    # @return [Object] the value now stored under the word's name
    def self.parse_command(h, c)
      h[c.to_sym] ||= true
    end

    # Records one --name[=value] flag. The value is everything after the
    # first "="; only {LIST_FLAGS} are split on commas (a single item stays
    # a String). A bare flag is +true+ and never replaces a value already
    # given for the same name; a valued flag replaces any earlier value, so
    # the last --timestamp=FMT wins. A flag with no name (+--+ or +--=x+)
    # echoes and is ignored.
    #
    # @param h [Hash{Symbol => Object}] options parsed so far; updated in place
    # @param f [String] the flag as typed, e.g. "--timestamp=%b %d, %Y %T"
    # @return [String, Array<String>, true, nil] the value now stored for the
    #   flag, or +nil+ when the flag has no name and is ignored
    def self.parse_flag(h, f)
      (name, val) = f[2..-1].split("=", 2)
      if name.nil? || name.empty?
        echo "#{f} has no option name, so it is ignored."
        return nil
      end

      key = name.to_sym
      if val.nil?
        h[key] ||= true
      elsif LIST_FLAGS.include?(key)
        val = val.split(",")

        h[key] = val.size == 1 ? val.first : val
      else
        h[key] = val
      end
    end

    # Parses the script's arguments into options. Arguments starting with
    # +--+ go through {parse_flag}; any other word goes through
    # {parse_command}. With no +args+, reads Script.current.vars: when
    # vars[0] holds a double quote, it is re-split so a quoted value
    # (--timestamp="%F %T %Z") stays one argument, which matters for
    # autostart, whose saved arguments reach vars[1..] split on spaces.
    #
    # @param args [Array<String>, nil] arguments to parse; +nil+ reads
    #   Script.current.vars
    # @return [OpenStruct] one member per option name; an option not given
    #   reads as +nil+
    def self.parse(args = nil)
      case args
      when nil
        if Script.current.vars[0].inspect =~ /\\"/
          args = Script.current.vars[0].scan(/[^\s"]*(?<!\\)"(?:\\"|[^"])+(?<!\\)"[^\s]*|(?:\\"|[^"\s])+/).collect { |s| s.gsub(/(?<!\\)"/, '').gsub('\\"', '"') }
        else
          args = Script.current.vars[1..-1]
        end
      end

      OpenStruct.new(**args.to_a.reduce(Hash.new) do |opts, v|
        if v.start_with?(FLAG_PREFIX)
          Opts.parse_flag(opts, v)
        else
          Opts.parse_command(opts, v)
        end
        opts
      end)
    end
  end

  # Turns the --lines option into a line count. Accepts whole numbers above
  # 0 written as plain digits or in groups of three digits split by "," or
  # "_" (50000, 50,000, 50_000); see {LINE_COUNT_PATTERN}. Anything else (0,
  # negative, words, misplaced separators like 50,00, a bare --lines or the
  # word lines) echoes one message and falls back to
  # {DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE}, so a bad value can neither crash the logger
  # nor make it rotate files without reading lines.
  #
  # @param value [String, true, nil] the parsed --lines option (+true+ for a
  #   bare --lines or the word lines, +nil+ when not given)
  # @return [Integer] lines per log file, always above 0
  def self.lines_per_file(value)
    return DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE if value.nil?

    count = Integer(value.delete(',_'), 10) if value.is_a?(String) && value.match?(LINE_COUNT_PATTERN)
    return count if count&.positive?

    problem = value.is_a?(String) ? "--lines=#{value} is not a valid line count" : 'lines was given without a value'
    echo "#{problem}; use a whole number above 0, like --lines=50000. Using the default of #{DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE} lines per file."
    DEFAULT_LINES_PER_FILE
  end

  # Whether a value can be handed to strftime as a timestamp format: only a
  # non-empty String is.
  #
  # @param value [Object] a --timestamp option or a log_timestamp_format setting
  # @return [Boolean]
  def self.timestamp_format?(value)
    value.is_a?(String) && !value.empty?
  end

  # Picks the timestamp format from the --timestamp option and the profile
  # settings, checking both the same way ({timestamp_format?}) so strftime
  # never sees anything but a format. A --timestamp=FMT wins. A --timestamp
  # with no format (bare flag, the word timestamp, or --timestamp=) echoes
  # once and is ignored. Settings that turn timestamps on without a usable
  # log_timestamp_format echo once and use {DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT}.
  #
  # @param option [String, true, nil] the parsed --timestamp option
  # @param enabled [Object] settings.log_timestamp; truthy turns timestamps on
  #   (+nil+ when there are no settings)
  # @param format [Object] settings.log_timestamp_format
  # @return [String, nil] the format to stamp with, or +nil+ for no timestamps
  def self.timestamp_format(option, enabled, format)
    return option if timestamp_format?(option)

    echo 'timestamp was given without a format, so it is ignored; to add timestamps use --timestamp="%F %T %Z".' unless option.nil?
    return nil unless enabled
    return format if timestamp_format?(format)

    echo "log_timestamp is on in your settings, but log_timestamp_format #{format.inspect} is not a format; using #{DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT.inspect}."
    DEFAULT_TIMESTAMP_FORMAT
  end

  # Starts loading the map in a background thread when nothing has loaded
  # it yet, so a Lich room number in --exclude or --rnum can be found. Lich
  # loads the map only when something asks for it, and in DragonRealms its
  # own room display does not by default. The load sends no game command,
  # but it can take seconds, so it runs off the logger's get loop: a logger
  # waiting on it during a login burst would let lich-5's 200-line
  # downstream buffer drop lines.
  #
  # @return [Thread, nil] the loading thread, or +nil+ when the map was
  #   already loaded and nothing was started
  def self.start_map_load
    return nil if Map.loaded?

    Thread.new do
      echo "no map database could be loaded, so Lich room numbers in --exclude and --rnum match no room; u<uid> entries still work." unless Map.load
    rescue StandardError => e
      echo "the map failed to load (#{e.message}), so Lich room numbers in --exclude and --rnum match no room; u<uid> entries still work."
    end
  end

  # Splits the --exclude option into game UID entries ("u7199") and Lich
  # room number entries ("288"). Empty entries (--exclude=,288) and a bare
  # --exclude (+true+) exclude nothing.
  #
  # @param option [String, Array<String>, true, nil] the parsed --exclude
  #   option
  # @return [Array(Array<String>, Array<String>)] the "u" UID entries, then
  #   the Lich room number entries
  def self.exclude_entries(option)
    Array(option).grep(String).reject(&:empty?).partition { |entry| entry.start_with?('u') }
  end

  # What one log format decides, as one frozen value. The logging loop
  # ({Logger}) is written once; everything that differs between output
  # formats is a field here, so a format is a value handed to the loop, not
  # a branch in it. There are two formats: text mode ({Mode::TEXT}) and XML
  # mode ({Mode::XML}, and {Mode::XML_STREAMS} for its streams option);
  # {Logger.mode_for} picks one for a run.
  #
  # The proc fields run in the logger's get loop, so they stay cheap and
  # keep no state: {#skip_line} on every line outside an excluded room, and
  # {#room_title} on those lines only while --rnum or --exclude tracks the
  # room. Both modes number a room title line with the one decorator,
  # {Logger.number_room_title}; {#uid_after_title} is the one thing they
  # tell it.
  #
  # Mode, {Mode::TEXT} and {Logger} are built new, not reopened, each time
  # Lich evaluates this module (once per log run in a session), and then
  # put in place of the last ones, with no constant warning. A run started
  # after log.lic was updated in the session gets the new definitions, and
  # a run already logging keeps the Mode value and the Logger class it
  # started with, and with the class its file, room and typed-command
  # helpers. A guarded `Mode ||=` would keep the first version's class for
  # the whole session, and a newer log.lic that adds a field would fail to
  # start. The constants are set only once each definition is whole, so a
  # run starting in another thread meanwhile (;force log racing a start)
  # gets the old definitions or the new ones, never a missing one.
  #
  # @!attribute [r] extension
  #   @return [String] the log file extension with its dot, e.g. ".log"
  # @!attribute [r] header_stamp
  #   @return [Proc, nil] makes the header line when timestamps are off:
  #     called with the Time, returns the line. +nil+ writes the same stamp
  #     as the footer (see {Logger.file_stamp}). With timestamps on,
  #     the header uses the timestamp format whatever this is.
  # @!attribute [r] upstream_line
  #   @return [Proc] called with one typed command as upstream_get returns
  #     it; returns the line logged for it
  # @!attribute [r] script_flags
  #   @return [Hash{Symbol => Boolean}] the Script.current flags the run
  #     sets when it starts, in order: +want_upstream: true+ sets
  #     Script.current.want_upstream = true. A flag not listed keeps
  #     lich-5's default.
  # @!attribute [r] skip_line
  #   @return [Proc] called with a game line; truthy when the line is not
  #     written (it then does not count toward --lines)
  # @!attribute [r] reget_line
  #   @return [Proc] called with one line of reget's answer, the timestamp
  #     format (+nil+ for none) and the login Time ($login_time); returns
  #     the line written at the top of the first file
  # @!attribute [r] room_title
  #   @return [Proc] called with a game line and the current room's title
  #     matcher ({Logger.room_title_pattern}; +nil+ when --rnum is off
  #     or before the first room title); truthy when the line is the room's
  #     title line, which --rnum numbers
  # @!attribute [r] uid_after_title
  #   @return [Boolean] where {Logger.number_room_title} puts the GemStone
  #     " (u<uid>)" on a room title line: +true+ right after the title's
  #     first "]", before any markup that follows it on the line ("</name>"
  #     in a Saga-identity <room> block); +false+ at the end of the line.
  # @!attribute [r] started_text
  #   @return [String] the echo once logging has started, before the log
  #     file's path (a space between them)
  mode = Data.define(:extension, :header_stamp, :upstream_line, :script_flags, :skip_line, :reget_line, :room_title,
                     :uid_after_title, :started_text)

  # Text mode, log.lic's format: the game text as lich-5 hands it to a
  # script (downstream text, lich-5's default, left on), Lich's echoes too
  # (want_script_output), and typed commands (want_upstream) as ">command"
  # with a leading "<c>" removed. Files end in ".log". A line that starts
  # with a stream, dialog or progress bar tag (script output can carry
  # them) is not written. Reget lines lose their "\r" ($_SERVERBUFFER_
  # keeps CRLF and reget splits on "\n") and get the $login_time stamp when
  # timestamps are on. They are not filtered: this mode never sets
  # want_downstream_xml, so reget has already removed every XML tag, and a
  # "<" left in a line is game text the server sent escaped as "&lt;"
  # (lich-5 lib/global_defs.rb, def reget). The room title line is the
  # one that starts with XMLData.room_title (see
  # {Logger.room_title_pattern}); its GemStone u-id goes at the end of the
  # line.
  mode::TEXT = mode.new(
    extension: '.log',
    header_stamp: nil,
    upstream_line: ->(command) { ">#{command.sub(/^<c>/, '')}" },
    script_flags: { want_script_output: true, want_upstream: true }.freeze,
    skip_line: ->(line) { line =~ %r{^</?(?:(?:push|pop)Stream|progressBar|dialogData|(?:open|close)Dialog)} },
    reget_line: lambda do |line, stamp_format, login_time|
      line = line.chomp
      stamp_format ? "#{login_time.strftime(stamp_format)}: #{line}" : line
    end,
    room_title: ->(line, title_pattern) { title_pattern&.match?(line.b) },
    uid_after_title: false,
    started_text: 'Non-XML Logging started, currently logging to'
  )

  # XML mode, logxml.lic's format (selected by --xml or by running as
  # ;logxml, see {Logger.mode_for}): the XML lich-5 hands a script that
  # wants the XML downstream and neither the text downstream nor script
  # output, and typed commands as "<!-- CLIENT -->command<!-- ENDCLIENT -->"
  # (a leading "<c>" kept). Files end in ".xml", and with timestamps off the
  # header is the date and a 12-hour time with the hour's leading zero
  # dropped ("2026-04-23 9:05am"); the footer is the same as text mode's. A
  # line that starts with pushStream or popStream (at the start of the line
  # or after a line break in it) is not written. The flags are set before
  # reget is called, so reget answers the raw XML the server sent; its lines
  # are written as they come, unstamped and unfiltered, less their "\r".
  #
  # --rnum numbers the room title line in the forms lich-5 numbers for its
  # own room display (lib/games.rb, process_room_information): at the start
  # of the line, an optional picture resource or popBold, then the roomName
  # style or a Saga-identity <room> block, such as
  # '<resource picture="0"/><style id="roomName" />[Title] (uid)' and
  # '<resource picture="0"/><room><name>[Title] (uid)</name>...'. Lich also
  # numbers those forms after a familiar pushStream, the familiar's view of
  # its own room, which is not the character's room, so this mode does not.
  # The form must start the String (\A, not ^): the numbers go at the first
  # "]", so a title after a line break in it is not numbered. The numbers
  # are {Logger.number_room_title}'s, as in text mode, with the GemStone
  # u-id right after the first "]", inside a <room> block's </name>.
  xml = mode.new(
    extension: '.xml',
    header_stamp: ->(time) { time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d %I:%M%P').sub(/0([0-9]+:)/, '\1') },
    upstream_line: ->(command) { "<!-- CLIENT -->#{command}<!-- ENDCLIENT -->" },
    script_flags: { want_script_output: false, want_upstream: true, want_downstream: false, want_downstream_xml: true }.freeze,
    skip_line: ->(line) { line =~ /^<(?:push|pop)Stream/ },
    reget_line: ->(line, _stamp_format, _login_time) { line.chomp },
    room_title: ->(line, _title_pattern) { line.match?(%r{\A(?:<resource picture="\d+"/>|<popBold/>)?(?:<style id="roomName"\s+/>|<room>)}) },
    uid_after_title: true,
    started_text: 'XML Logging started, currently logging to'
  )
  # Set with const_set, as YARD cannot resolve a constant set on a local.
  mode.const_set(:XML, xml)

  # XML mode started with the word streams or --streams: every line is
  # written, the pushStream and popStream lines too.
  mode.const_set(:XML_STREAMS, mode::XML.with(skip_line: ->(_line) { false }))

  # One log run, in one {Mode}: all its state (options, settings, the room
  # it is in, the file it writes) is in this object, never on the module.
  # {GameLogger.main} builds one per run of the script and runs it.
  #
  # The logic that decides what a line becomes ({#skip_line?},
  # {#decorate_title}, {#format_line}, {#reget_lines}) is in small methods
  # that read their arguments, the run's settings, its current room and
  # XMLData.game. Every time the run uses comes from the +clock+ given to
  # {#initialize}, and the game lines, reget, the start echo and the
  # settings come from its +io+, so a spec can feed a run lines and a time
  # without stubbing Time. The rest still goes through Lich: the echoes
  # from {GameLogger.timestamp_format}, {GameLogger.lines_per_file},
  # {GameLogger.start_map_load} and {Logger.upstream_stopped}; the typed
  # commands ({Logger.start_upstream_logger}); and $login_time, XMLData,
  # Script.current and Map.
  #
  # The helpers a run calls while it logs (the file, room and
  # typed-command methods below) are this class's own methods, called on
  # the run's class, so a later evaluation of log.lic, whatever its version,
  # cannot change them under a run already logging. The module methods a
  # run calls only while it starts ({GameLogger.timestamp_format},
  # {GameLogger.lines_per_file}, {GameLogger.exclude_entries},
  # {GameLogger.start_map_load}) stay on the module.
  #
  # The class is defined in a throwaway module and set as Logger below,
  # once it is whole (see {Mode}); the name it is set to is its name.
  fresh = Module.new
  class fresh::Logger
    # The line written after the reget lines at the top of the first file.
    REGET_MARKER = "<!-- Above contents from reget; full logging now active -->\n"

    # A run started less than this many seconds after login ($login_time)
    # writes reget's lines at the top of its first file.
    REGET_WINDOW = 30

    # The header and footer stamp when timestamps are off: the date, the
    # time to the millisecond and the UTC offset. A constant of this class
    # is safe from the session: each evaluation of log.lic builds a new
    # class, while a guarded constant on the module would keep the first
    # loaded value for the session.
    FILE_STAMP_FORMAT = '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S.%L %:z'

    # The script name that selects XML mode: logxml.lic loads this file, so
    # a run started as ;logxml runs under this name (see {mode_for}).
    XML_SCRIPT_NAME = 'logxml'

    # Picks the {Mode} for a run: XML mode when the options hold --xml (or
    # the word xml) or the script runs as {XML_SCRIPT_NAME}, with every line
    # kept ({XML_STREAMS_MODE}) when they also hold the word streams or
    # --streams, and text mode ({DEFAULT_MODE}) otherwise; text mode ignores
    # streams. The modes are this class's own, built with it, so a run never
    # gets a Mode from another evaluation of log.lic.
    #
    # @param options [OpenStruct] the run's options, as {Opts.parse} returns
    #   them; reads +xml+ and +streams+
    # @param script_name [String, nil] the name the script was started as
    #   (Script.current.name), e.g. "log" or "logxml"
    # @return [Mode] {DEFAULT_MODE}, {XML_MODE} or {XML_STREAMS_MODE}
    def self.mode_for(options, script_name)
      return DEFAULT_MODE unless options.xml || script_name == XML_SCRIPT_NAME

      options.streams ? XML_STREAMS_MODE : XML_MODE
    end

    # The name the running script was started as ("log", or "logxml" for
    # XML mode run by logxml.lic), which starts the logger's own lines in the
    # log file and in the Lich log, so they name the logger to look at.
    #
    # @return [String] Script.current.name, or "log" when there is no
    #   current script
    def self.log_label
      Script.current&.name || 'log'
    end

    # Looks up the Lich room number for a game room UID in the map's UID
    # table. Map.ids_from_uid is a plain hash lookup: unlike Room.current and
    # Map.current it never matches the room by text, so it cannot send
    # LOCATION or PEER from the logger (which would clear the logger's own
    # buffer) and costs the same in rooms the map cannot place. It never
    # loads the map either (see {GameLogger.start_map_load}): until the map
    # is loaded, or learns the UID, every room has no Lich number here.
    #
    # @param uid [Integer] the game's room UID (XMLData.room_id)
    # @return [Integer, nil] the one Lich room number mapped to +uid+, or
    #   +nil+ when the UID maps to no room or to more than one
    def self.lich_room_id(uid)
      ids = Map.ids_from_uid(uid)
      ids.first if ids.size == 1
    end

    # Builds the matcher for the current room's title line, for --rnum. In
    # DragonRealms lich-5 wraps the bracketed title from the room subtitle in
    # one more pair of brackets (XMLData.room_title is "[[Title]]" while the
    # game line reads "[Title] (230008)"), so one pair is removed first.
    #
    # The pattern is built from the title's bytes, so match it against
    # +line.b+: a UTF-8 pattern with letters outside ASCII raises
    # Encoding::CompatibilityError on a binary game line with high bytes.
    #
    # @param title [String] XMLData.room_title
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @return [Regexp, nil] a pattern matching the bytes of a line that
    #   starts with the title, or +nil+ when the title is empty or not
    #   "[...]" shaped (before the first room title arrives), so no line is
    #   changed
    def self.room_title_pattern(title, game)
      title = title.to_s
      title = title[1..-2] if game.to_s.start_with?('DR') && title.start_with?('[[') && title.end_with?(']]')
      return nil unless title.length > 2 && title.start_with?('[') && title.end_with?(']')

      /\A#{Regexp.escape(title.b)}/
    end

    # Adds the room numbers to a room title line, for --rnum, in either
    # mode: " - <number>" inside the first "]" when the room has a Lich
    # number, and in GemStone " (u<uid>)" unless the line already shows
    # "(<uid>)" (the game can send its room id after the title, as in
    # "[Sanctum of Scales, Columns] (4216015)", and a second one would say
    # the same twice). The u-id goes at the end of the line, or right after
    # the first "]" with +uid_after_title+ (XML mode, where markup such as
    # "</name>" follows the title). A room with no Lich number keeps its
    # title as the game sent it (apart from the GemStone UID). DragonRealms
    # titles never get a u-id.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line whose first "]" closes the room title
    # @param lich_id [Integer, nil] the room's Lich number, see {lich_room_id}
    # @param uid [Integer] the game's room UID (XMLData.room_id)
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @param uid_after_title [Boolean] true puts the u-id right after the
    #   first "]", false (the default, text mode) at the end of the line; see
    #   {Mode#uid_after_title}
    # @return [String] a new String with the numbers added, or +line+ itself
    #   when there is nothing to add
    def self.number_room_title(line, lich_id, uid, game, uid_after_title: false)
      line = line.sub(']') { " - #{lich_id}]" } if lich_id
      return line unless game.to_s.start_with?('GS') && !line.include?("(#{uid})")

      uid_after_title ? line.sub(']') { "] (u#{uid})" } : "#{line} (u#{uid})"
    end

    # The folder that holds every character's log folders: Lich's LOG_DIR,
    # which Lich's --logs= option sets (and which pilepull.lic reads these
    # logs from), or LICH_DIR/logs when LOG_DIR is not defined or empty.
    #
    # @return [String] the logs folder
    def self.log_root
      root = (LOG_DIR if defined?(LOG_DIR)).to_s
      root.empty? ? File.join(LICH_DIR, 'logs') : root
    end

    # The folder for one character's logs in one month:
    # <log root>/<game>-<name>/<YYYY>/<MM>, see {log_root}.
    #
    # @param game [String] XMLData.game, e.g. "GSIV" or "DR"
    # @param name [String] the character's name (XMLData.name)
    # @param time [Time] the time the log file is started
    # @return [String] the folder path (not created here)
    def self.log_directory(game, name, time)
      File.join(log_root, "#{game}-#{name}", time.strftime('%Y'), time.strftime('%m'))
    end

    # The path for a new log file: <dir>/<YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS><extension>.
    # The first file of a run keeps that name even when the file exists (it
    # is appended to, as a restarted logger always has). A file started by
    # rotation never reuses an existing name: when a whole file filled within
    # one second (a tiny --lines during a burst), or another logger wrote
    # that name, "_01", "_02", ... is added before the extension. That
    # suffix sorts after the first file's name and, up to _99, in order, so a
    # month folder listed by name keeps the files in the order written.
    #
    # @param dir [String] the folder, see {log_directory}
    # @param time [Time] the time the file is started
    # @param extension [String] the file extension with its dot, e.g. ".log"
    # @param rotated [Boolean] true for a file started by rotation (a new day
    #   or the --lines count), false for the first file of a run
    # @return [String] the file path
    def self.log_file_path(dir, time, extension, rotated:)
      stem = File.join(dir, time.strftime('%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S'))
      path = "#{stem}#{extension}"
      return path unless rotated

      suffix = 0
      path = "#{stem}_#{format('%02d', suffix += 1)}#{extension}" while File.exist?(path)
      path
    end

    # The header or footer line of a log file: the time now in the
    # timestamp format, or in {FILE_STAMP_FORMAT} (date, time to the
    # millisecond and UTC offset) when timestamps are off.
    #
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format, or +nil+ for no
    #   timestamps
    # @param clock [#now] where the time comes from; Time by default (a spec
    #   can pass a fixed clock)
    # @return [String] the line, without a line end
    def self.file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: Time)
      clock.now.strftime(stamp_format || FILE_STAMP_FORMAT)
    end

    # Opens a log file for appending, writes its header line, runs the block
    # with it, then writes its footer line and closes it. The footer is
    # written exactly once, from an ensure, so a file gets one also when the
    # logger stops (;kill, logout, Lich exiting) and not only when it starts a
    # new file; see {write_footer}. A file whose header could not be written
    # gets no footer, and is still closed, so an error in the header never
    # leaves the file open. A close that fails goes to the Lich log with the
    # file's path and the error, and is otherwise ignored. Lines are written
    # as they come (sync is on), but a close that fails can still mean a
    # missing footer or a write error the system reported late.
    #
    # @param filename [String] the log file path; an existing file is
    #   appended to
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format for the header and
    #   footer, or +nil+ for the default, see {file_stamp}
    # @param clock [#now] where the header and footer times come from; Time
    #   by default
    # @param header_stamp [Proc, nil] used for the header line instead of
    #   {file_stamp}'s default when +stamp_format+ is +nil+: called with the
    #   time, returns the line (see {Mode#header_stamp}); +nil+ uses the
    #   default, as the footer always does
    # @yield [file] writes the file's lines
    # @yieldparam file [File] the open log file, with sync on
    # @yieldreturn [void]
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] when the file cannot be opened, its
    #   header cannot be written, or the block returns (the file is full or
    #   the day changed) and the footer cannot be written; the file is closed
    #   first. Errors raised by the block pass through after the footer and
    #   close.
    def self.with_log_file(filename, stamp_format, clock: Time, header_stamp: nil)
      file = File.open(filename, 'a')
      header_written = false
      finished = false
      begin
        file.sync = true
        file.puts(stamp_format.nil? && header_stamp ? header_stamp.call(clock.now) : file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: clock))
        header_written = true
        yield file
        finished = true
      ensure
        begin
          # Not finished: an error or Thread#kill (;kill) is stopping the logger.
          write_footer(file, stamp_format, stopping: !finished, clock: clock) if header_written
        ensure
          begin
            file.close
          rescue StandardError => e
            Lich.log "#{log_label}: closing the log file #{filename} failed (#{e.class}: #{e.message}); lines are written as they come, with sync on, but the file may be missing its footer or a last write"
          end
        end
      end
    end

    # Writes a log file's footer line. It runs each time a file is closed:
    # when a new file starts (a new day or the --lines count), where a failed
    # write raises, and when the logger stops, where a failed write (disk
    # full, a closed file) goes to the Lich log and is otherwise ignored, so
    # it does not replace the reason the logger stopped.
    #
    # @param file [File] the open log file
    # @param stamp_format [String, nil] the strftime format, or +nil+ for
    #   the default, see {file_stamp}
    # @param stopping [Boolean] true when the logger is stopping (an error or
    #   ;kill), false when the file is closed for the next one
    # @param clock [#now] where the footer time comes from; Time by default
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [IOError, SystemCallError] when the write fails and +stopping+ is
    #   false
    def self.write_footer(file, stamp_format, stopping:, clock: Time)
      file.puts file_stamp(stamp_format, clock: clock)
    rescue IOError, SystemCallError => e
      raise unless stopping

      Lich.log "#{log_label}: the footer of #{file.path} was not written while the logger stopped (#{e.class}: #{e.message})"
      nil
    end

    # Starts the thread that logs typed commands: each command lich-5 hands
    # the script's upstream buffer is pushed onto its downstream buffer as
    # the block formats it, by default as ">command" (a leading "<c>"
    # removed, the {Mode#upstream_line} of {DEFAULT_MODE}), so the get loop
    # in {#run} writes it in order with the game lines. The format comes
    # as a block, not an argument, so a wrapper that calls +super+ with no
    # arguments passes it on. When the thread stops on an error, it
    # says why and leaves a notice in the log file, see {upstream_stopped}.
    # Thread#kill (;kill, logout) stops it without either, and so does a
    # StopIteration from upstream_get or the push: Kernel#loop rescues that
    # one itself and ends the loop. lich-5's upstream_get does not raise it.
    #
    # @yield [command] formats one typed command for the log
    # @yieldparam command [String] the command as upstream_get returns it
    # @yieldreturn [String] the line to log
    # @return [Thread] the started thread
    def self.start_upstream_logger(&format_command)
      format_command ||= DEFAULT_MODE.upstream_line
      Thread.new do
        loop { Script.current.downstream_buffer.push format_command.call(upstream_get) }
      rescue StandardError => e
        upstream_stopped(e)
      end
    end

    # Says that typed commands are no longer logged because the upstream
    # thread (see {start_upstream_logger}) stopped on +error+: echoes the
    # error's class and message, the file and line it was raised at (no
    # directory, see {upstream_frame}) and how to restart the logger with the
    # arguments it was started with, and pushes one comment line onto the
    # downstream buffer, so the log file shows where the typed command lines
    # end. The comment starts with the name the logger runs as (see
    # {log_label}). The game lines are still logged. That line goes through
    # the get loop like a game line: it is stamped when timestamps are on,
    # and it is not written while the logger is in an --exclude room.
    #
    # Text mode sets want_script_output, so in lich-5 the echo reaches the
    # downstream buffer too and is logged just before the notice line; XML
    # mode does not, and logs only the notice line.
    #
    # @param error [StandardError] the error that stopped the thread
    # @return [void]
    def self.upstream_stopped(error)
      reason = "#{error.class}: #{error.message.to_s.gsub(/\s+/, ' ').strip}"
      where = upstream_frame(error)
      script = Script.current
      restart = [";#{script.name}", script.vars&.first.to_s.strip].reject(&:empty?).join(' ')
      echo "typed commands are no longer logged: the upstream thread stopped on #{reason}#{" at #{where}" if where}. " \
           "Game lines are still logged; restart the logger (;kill #{script.name}, then #{restart}) to log typed commands again."
      # A "-" before another "-" is spaced out: "--" would end the comment
      # early, and is not allowed inside an XML comment. The ")" after the
      # reason keeps a last "-" off the closing "-->".
      script.downstream_buffer.push "<!-- #{log_label}: typed commands are not logged after this line; the upstream thread stopped (#{reason.gsub(/-(?=-)/, '- ')}) -->"
    end

    # The file name and line +error+ was raised at, from its first backtrace
    # frame without the directory, which keeps the echo short. (The log file
    # still shows the full log path, in the "currently logging to" echo.)
    #
    # @param error [StandardError]
    # @return [String, nil] "file:line", the frame as is when it has no
    #   "path:line" start, or nil when the error has no backtrace
    def self.upstream_frame(error)
      frame = error.backtrace&.first
      return nil if frame.nil?

      match = frame.match(/\A(.+?):(\d+)/)
      match ? "#{File.basename(match[1])}:#{match[2]}" : frame
    end

    # Sets up a run; nothing is read, set or started until {#run}.
    #
    # @param options [OpenStruct] the run's options, as {Opts.parse} returns
    #   them
    # @param mode [Mode] the log format; by default {DEFAULT_MODE}, the
    #   {Mode::TEXT} built with this class
    # @param clock [#now] where every time the run uses comes from (file
    #   names and folders, the new-day check, line stamps, the reget window,
    #   the header and footer); Time by default
    # @param io [Object] answers Lich's script helpers +get+ (every game
    #   line), +reget+, +echo+ (the start echo only) and, when it has one,
    #   +get_settings+. They are called with send, as Lich defines them as
    #   private top-level methods. GameLogger by default, which reaches
    #   them in Lich (and which the spec harness gives stand-ins). The
    #   other echoes still go to Lich's echo, and typed commands do not
    #   come through +io+: {Logger.start_upstream_logger} reads
    #   Lich's upstream_get and pushes each command into
    #   Script.current.downstream_buffer, which Lich's get reads, so with
    #   an +io+ whose +get+ reads elsewhere typed commands are not logged.
    def initialize(options, mode: DEFAULT_MODE, clock: Time, io: GameLogger)
      @options = options
      @mode = mode
      @clock = clock
      @io = io
    end

    # Runs the logger until the script is killed: starts a new log file on
    # each new day, with the first line read after midnight (excluded or
    # not), and after the --lines count of written lines (see
    # {GameLogger.lines_per_file}; excluded and filtered lines do not
    # count), when the next line is read, so the next file is named and
    # dated by its first line; stamps lines when
    # {GameLogger.timestamp_format} returns a format. The file header and
    # footer carry that format, or the date and time when there is none; a
    # file also gets its footer when the logger stops (see
    # {Logger.with_log_file}). Typed commands are logged as the mode's
    # {Mode#upstream_line} makes them (see
    # {Logger.start_upstream_logger}), lines seen in --exclude rooms
    # are skipped, and --rnum adds the room number to room titles.
    #
    # The room for --exclude and --rnum is read from XMLData and the map's
    # UID table (see {Logger.lich_room_id}), never from Room.current or
    # Map.current. When a Lich number is needed, the map is loaded once in
    # the background (see {GameLogger.start_map_load}). A found Lich number
    # is kept until the room changes; while there is none, or while that
    # load is still running, each line asks the map again, so a room is
    # numbered and excluded from the line after the map learns its UID.
    #
    # @note A line is checked against the room the parser holds when the
    #   logger reads the line, not when the game sent it. When the logger
    #   falls behind a room change, a room title can be left without numbers
    #   or get the next room's numbers, and lines at the edge of an excluded
    #   room can be skipped or logged on the wrong side.
    # @note Lines read before the map has loaded are logged even in a room
    #   excluded by Lich number (a u<uid> entry needs no map).
    # @note When another script reloads the map, the UID table is cleared
    #   and rebuilt one room at a time. A UID shared by several rooms can
    #   then show just one of them, and the logger keeps that Lich number
    #   for the rest of the room. Only the logger's own load is waited out.
    # @note Lich evaluates `module GameLogger` again for every run in the
    #   session (;force log). That replaces {Logger} and {Mode} (see
    #   {Mode}), and a run already logging keeps its own class, with the
    #   file, room and typed-command helpers it calls while it logs (such as
    #   {Logger.log_file_path} and {Logger.with_log_file}). What it reads
    #   only while it starts is still shared: the module methods each later
    #   run redefines ({GameLogger.timestamp_format},
    #   {GameLogger.lines_per_file}, {GameLogger.exclude_entries},
    #   {GameLogger.start_map_load}, {Opts}) and the `||=` constants, which
    #   keep the first run's values for the session.
    # @return [void]
    def run
      configure
      self.class.start_upstream_logger(&@mode.upstream_line)
      @started = false
      # The line that opens the next file. A line read after midnight belongs
      # to the new day's file: it is kept here while the old file is closed.
      # After a full file, the next line is read before the next file is
      # opened, so its name and date are those of its first line: a file
      # opened at once would wait with no line, and when that line came after
      # midnight it would go to the new day's file, leaving an old-day file
      # with only its header and footer.
      @carried_line = nil
      loop { log_next_file }
    end

    # Whether a game line is dropped by the mode's filter
    # ({Mode#skip_line}). Room exclusion is checked separately.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line as get returns it
    # @return [Boolean] true when the line is not written
    def skip_line?(line)
      @mode.skip_line.call(line) ? true : false
    end

    # Adds the room numbers to +line+ when --rnum is on and the mode says it
    # is the current room's title line ({Mode#room_title}), with
    # {Logger.number_room_title}, the decorator both modes share, placing
    # the GemStone u-id where the mode's {Mode#uid_after_title} says. With
    # --rnum off no line is numbered, also when --exclude tracks the room.
    #
    # @param line [String] a game line
    # @return [String] the line with the room numbers added, or +line+
    #   itself when it is not the title line (or --rnum is off)
    def decorate_title(line)
      return line unless @show_room_numbers && @mode.room_title.call(line, @title_pattern)

      self.class.number_room_title(line, @lich_id, @room_uid, XMLData.game, uid_after_title: @mode.uid_after_title)
    end

    # The text written to the file for one line: "<time>: <line>" in the
    # run's timestamp format, or the line as it is when timestamps are off.
    #
    # @param line [String] the line to write
    # @param time [Time] the time to stamp it with
    # @return [String] the text, without a line end
    def format_line(line, time)
      @stamp_format ? "#{time.strftime(@stamp_format)}: #{line}" : line
    end

    # reget's answer as the lines written at the top of the first file,
    # each formatted by the mode's {Mode#reget_line}.
    #
    # @param lines [Array<String>, nil] reget's answer; +nil+ (an empty
    #   buffer) gives no lines
    # @param login_time [Time] when the character logged in ($login_time)
    # @return [Array<String>] the lines to write, without line ends
    def reget_lines(lines, login_time)
      Array(lines).map { |line| @mode.reget_line.call(line, @stamp_format, login_time) }
    end

    private

    # Reads the settings and options and sets the script's flags, in the
    # order the logger always has: get_settings, the mode's
    # {Mode#script_flags}, then the timestamp format and the --lines count
    # (each may echo), then the map load when a Lich number is needed. Every
    # value goes on this run.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def configure
      if @io.respond_to?(:get_settings, true)
        settings = @io.send(:get_settings)
        stamp_enable = settings.log_timestamp
        stamp_format = settings.log_timestamp_format
      end
      @mode.script_flags.each { |flag, value| Script.current.public_send(:"#{flag}=", value) }
      @stamp_format = GameLogger.timestamp_format(@options.timestamp, stamp_enable, stamp_format)
      @show_room_numbers = @options.roomnum || @options.rnum
      @lines_per_file = GameLogger.lines_per_file(@options.lines)
      configure_rooms
    end

    # Works out which room lookups the run needs from --exclude and --rnum,
    # starts the map load when a Lich number is needed, and clears the
    # room state, so the first line works the room out.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def configure_rooms
      @uid_excludes, @id_excludes = GameLogger.exclude_entries(@options.exclude)
      @look_up_lich_id = @show_room_numbers || !@id_excludes.empty?
      @track_room = @look_up_lich_id || !@uid_excludes.empty?
      @map_loader = (GameLogger.start_map_load if @look_up_lich_id)
      @room_count = @room_uid = @room_title = @title_pattern = @lich_id = nil
      @room_uid_excluded = @room_id_excluded = false
    end

    # Opens the next log file (in the month's folder, created when
    # missing), writes the reget and the start echo when it is the run's
    # first file, and fills it with lines until it is full or the day
    # changes. The first file of a run takes its name from the time it
    # starts; every later one is opened when its first line has been read.
    #
    # @return [void]
    # @raise [SystemCallError, IOError] see {Logger.with_log_file}
    def log_next_file
      @carried_line = read_line if @started && @carried_line.nil?
      dir = self.class.log_directory(XMLData.game, XMLData.name, @clock.now)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dir) unless File.exist?(dir)
      date = today
      filename = self.class.log_file_path(dir, @clock.now, @mode.extension, rotated: @started)
      self.class.with_log_file(filename, @stamp_format, clock: @clock, header_stamp: @mode.header_stamp) do |file|
        start_logging(file, filename) unless @started
        fill_file(file, date)
      end
    end

    # Writes reget's lines and the reget marker at the top of the run's
    # first file when the run started within {REGET_WINDOW} seconds of
    # login, then echoes where the log goes.
    #
    # @param file [File] the run's first log file
    # @param filename [String] its path, for the echo
    # @return [void]
    def start_logging(file, filename)
      if (@clock.now - $login_time) < REGET_WINDOW
        reget_lines(@io.send(:reget), $login_time).each { |line| file.puts line }
        file.puts REGET_MARKER
      end

      @io.send(:echo, "#{@mode.started_text} #{filename}")
      @started = true
    end

    # Writes lines to +file+ until --lines lines are written, or until a
    # line is read on a day other than +date+; that line is kept for the
    # next file. Lines in an excluded room and lines the mode drops are read
    # and not written, and do not count.
    #
    # @param file [File] the open log file
    # @param date [String] the file's day, "%Y-%m-%d"
    # @return [void]
    def fill_file(file, date)
      written = 0
      while written < @lines_per_file
        line = @carried_line || read_line
        @carried_line = nil
        # The date is checked before anything else, on every line read,
        # excluded or not, so the first line of a new day starts its file.
        if today != date
          @carried_line = line
          break
        end
        if @track_room
          refresh_room
          refresh_lich_id
          next if room_excluded?

          line = decorate_title(line)
        end
        next if skip_line?(line)

        file.puts format_line(line, @clock.now)
        written += 1
      end
    end

    # Works the room out again when the parser has moved on (room count,
    # UID or title changed): whether its UID is excluded and, for --rnum,
    # its title matcher. The Lich number is cleared, for
    # {#refresh_lich_id} to find.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def refresh_room
      return if XMLData.room_count == @room_count && XMLData.room_id == @room_uid && XMLData.room_title == @room_title

      @room_count = XMLData.room_count
      @room_uid = XMLData.room_id
      @room_title = XMLData.room_title.dup
      @room_uid_excluded = @uid_excludes.include?("u#{@room_uid}")
      @room_id_excluded = false
      @lich_id = nil
      @title_pattern = (self.class.room_title_pattern(@room_title, XMLData.game) if @show_room_numbers)
    end

    # Looks the room's Lich number up and checks it against the --exclude
    # Lich numbers, when one is needed and the room's UID is not already
    # excluded. Only a found number is kept for the room. Until then the map
    # is asked again on each line, as it may not be loaded yet or may learn
    # the UID later. While the logger's own map load runs, a found number is
    # asked for again too: the UID table is rebuilt one room at a time, so a
    # UID shared by several rooms can show one.
    #
    # @return [void]
    def refresh_lich_id
      return unless @look_up_lich_id && !@room_uid_excluded && (@lich_id.nil? || @map_loader&.alive?)

      @lich_id = self.class.lich_room_id(@room_uid)
      @room_id_excluded = !@lich_id.nil? && @id_excludes.include?(@lich_id.to_s)
    end

    # Whether the current room is excluded, by its UID or its Lich number.
    #
    # @return [Boolean]
    def room_excluded?
      @room_uid_excluded || @room_id_excluded
    end

    # The day now, as the file names write it.
    #
    # @return [String] "%Y-%m-%d"
    def today
      @clock.now.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')
    end

    # The next game line, from Lich's get (blocks until there is one).
    #
    # @return [String]
    def read_line
      @io.send(:get)
    end
  end

  # The {Mode} built with this Logger ({Mode::TEXT}), for a run built with
  # no mode and for typed commands started with no format. It is the
  # class's own, so a run never gets a Mode from another evaluation.
  fresh::Logger::DEFAULT_MODE = mode::TEXT

  # XML mode built with this Logger ({Mode::XML}), see {Logger.mode_for}.
  fresh::Logger.const_set(:XML_MODE, mode::XML)

  # XML mode keeping every line ({Mode::XML_STREAMS}), built with this
  # Logger, see {Logger.mode_for}.
  fresh::Logger.const_set(:XML_STREAMS_MODE, mode::XML_STREAMS)

  # Puts this evaluation's Mode and Logger in place of the last ones. Each
  # name is set once, to a whole definition, so it always reads as the old
  # or the new one. Setting a constant again warns unless $VERBOSE is nil,
  # so it is nil for the two assignments only.
  begin
    verbose = $VERBOSE
    $VERBOSE = nil
    const_set(:Mode, mode)
    const_set(:Logger, fresh::Logger)
  ensure
    $VERBOSE = verbose
  end

  # Runs the logger for this run of the script until the script is killed:
  # one {Logger} with +options+, in the mode {Logger.mode_for} picks from
  # them and the script's name (text mode, or XML mode for --xml or a run
  # started as ;logxml); see {Logger#run}.
  #
  # @param options [OpenStruct] this run's options, as {Opts.parse}
  #   returns them; parsed from the script's arguments when not given
  # @return [void]
  def self.main(options = Opts.parse)
    logger = Logger
    logger.new(options, mode: logger.mode_for(options, Script.current.name)).run
  end
end

GameLogger.main
